# One member of a group is enough to make the merged loop impossible.
#
# `first` and `second` agree: both take an Int and give a String. But
# `third` is in the same cycle and takes a String, so the one slot the
# three would share cannot hold what all of them put in it. The pass
# merges on arity alone -- all three take one argument -- and the
# checker is left with a loop that carries an Int one way round and a
# String the other.
#
# The lesson is not that this program is wrong: with the pass off it
# checks and runs, which its `.notail.ast.out` shows. It is that a
# merged group needs its members to agree about more than how many
# arguments they take, and only the checker can see that far. See
# docs/optimization.md, "The groups this cannot make work".
#
# This program is expected to FAIL type checking with the pass on. Its
# `.ast.out` holds the error message.

def first(n: Int) -> String:
    if n == 0:
        "done"
    else:
        second(n - 1)


def second(n: Int) -> String:
    if n == 0:
        "also done"
    else:
        third("mark")


def third(mark: String) -> String:
    if mark == "":
        "empty"
    else:
        first(0)


print(first(3))
